\begin_layout Abstract
In this thesis, a novel technique to create interpretable reinforcement
 learning policies is introduced.
 From an interpretability perspective, it is advantageous if solutions resemble
 known and familiar strategies.
 Therefore, we propose to use genetic programming to enhance a previously
 developed domain expert strategy, by elaborating essential algorithmic
 and structural improvements, instead of starting from scratch.
 To achieve this, the tree edit distance between the program's computation
 tree and a genetic improvement is calculated and used as a complexity metric.
 Additionally, the option to select a fixed core in the expert program is
 implemented, which is not affected in the evolutionary process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The approach is tested on the mountain car and industrial benchmark by performin
g a symbolic regression on transition samples that were collected by black
 box agents.
 The tests include over 100 combinations of agents, policies and other aspects,
 which are compared to regular runs.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
The evaluation shows that the familiarity paradigm has no disadvantages
 compared to regular GP, but is easier to interpret.
 Problems with very small programs, in which a few edits can have fundamental
 effects, can be solved by using fixed core structures.
 The black box agent of the industrial benchmark could not be sufficiently
 approximated, yet familiar candidates with equivalent performance are found.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Further constraints on the search space (e.g., fixed nodes, limited set of
 operators, action space constraint) do not significantly restrict offline
 performance, but even induce comparatively good online performance.
 Therefore, the presented approach offers many possibilities, both for genetic
 programming and as a way to find more easily interpretable policies.
\end_layout

