\begin_layout Section
Interpretable Machine Learning
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Interpretable-Machine-Learning"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast to performance, interpretability has many manifestations and
 is not easy to determine.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, Doshi-Velez and Kim propose a subdivision of interpretability into various
 relevant factors, including the following considerations: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
How is the problem formulation incomplete?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
At what level is the evaluation being performed? (application, general user
 study, proxy)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are task-related relevant factors? (e.g.
 global vs.
 local, severity of incompleteness, level of user expertise, time constraints)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
What are method-related relevant factors being explored? (e.g.
 form of cognitive chunks, number of cognitive chunks, compositionality,
 monotonicity, uncertainty)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this thesis, we deal with RL problems, which are incomplete as they are
 not fully testable and can be solved in various ways.
 The level of evaluation is also not limited, as applications can also be
 learned through proxy models.
 Considering the task-related relevant factors, we are particularly interested
 in global interpretability, as post-hoc explanations for a subset of decisions
 is not sufficient to understand policies that depend on a sequential MDP.
  As claimed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI-unified-approach"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

The best explanation of a simple model is the model itself; it perfectly
 represents itself and is easy to understand.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 To find a model that is its own best representation, candidate solutions
 can be searched directly in a feasible region that is constrained to be
 interpretable.
 Computer programs or, in a larger context, Turing complete languages are
 the interface for explicit communication between machines and humans that
 will be used in this work.
 An evolutionary method of finding such policies is genetic programming,
 which will be further presented in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset

.
 We argue that simple and interpretable policies can often seem like arbitrary
 combinations of operators, as the solution path often deviates strongly
 from the strategy that an expert would choose.
 Understanding those requires a lot of effort, which is not clearly measurable.
 Doshi-Velez and Kim 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "XAI_doshivelez2017rigorous"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 define a cognitive chunk as the basic unit of explanation.
 Complexity measures for formulae in genetic programming only consider the
 number of mathematical components of a formula (number of chunks), and
 the complexity of the components (form of cognitive chunks).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interpretable machine learning methods often rely on supplying simple results
 that can be understood by many people.
 However, if the prior knowledge of an expert can already be assumed, an
 explanation can be far more specific (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:The-interpretability-gap"

\end_inset

).
 The same strategy is used in human feedback methods, where the level of
 user experience is taken into account to focus on innovations rather than
 explaining every aspect.
 Therefore, preserving familiar chunks can serve as a trusted anchor point
 for further attachments.
 In contrast to machine teaching, where techniques are investigated how
 the human can facilitate the learning of models, here the machine is supposed
 to lecture the human.
 
\end_layout

