\begin_layout Section
Related Work
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Related-Work"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Industrial control strategies feature many peculiarities which can now be
 addressed with reinforcement learning, such as highly dimensional 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "siemens-gas-turbine-rl-nn"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 or high stochasticity 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "PSONN_fuzzy_RL_Hein_Udluft,siemens-rl-stochastic-bayesian-nn"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, which are not interpretable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite much interest, only little work covers the problems of finding interpret
able policies.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Hein_Udluft_Runkler_interpretable_gp"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, Hein et al.
 present a method which uses genetic programming to create holistically
 interpretable policies for industrial controllers.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hein_fuzzy_gp"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, the possibility of deriving a fuzzy rule set for more complex problems
 is presented.
 The possibility of training on a surrogate model, such as performing a
 symbolic regression on previously collected transition samples is evaluated
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, which, considering some limitations allows for a secure learning process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Despite their simplicity, the generated formulas are often hardly comprehensible
 because they are too mathematical and contain no recognizable intuition.
 We argue that it is advantageous when solutions resemble previously known
 strategies.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Tresp_prior_knowledge"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, it is shown how prior knowledge in the form of a set of rules can be used
 to pre-structure policies that provide improved trustworthiness and allow
 a probabilistic interpretation.
 A similar method is evaluated in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "residual-dynamic-learning"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, where human control strategies in an industrial area are enhanced with
 machine learning.
 These approaches allow a stochastic version of interpretability, but not
 in a global manner such that novelties can be easily recognized.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
FloatBarrier
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Familiar Genetic Programming 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Familiar-Genetic-Programming"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following, the implementation of the approach to enhance genetic
 programming with familiarity is presented.
 The prerequisites are an expert solution and transition samples from a
 machine learning solution with better performance, which can be used for
 a symbolic regression (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "Symbolic-Regression"

\end_inset

).
 The selection process of the data is further described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Offline-Data-Selection"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, the domain expert's program must be represented as computational
 tree, which functions as genetic ancestor for all programs (origin).
 If possible, the hierarchical structure should be arranged so that the
 more important structural components are closer to the root node.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:Familiarity-Measurement-TED"

\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Tree Edit Distance} (TED)
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is proposed as metric to calculate the amount of required changes to the
 origin tree.
 Particular features of the GP suites implemented and described in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:GP-Suite-Implementation"

\end_inset

.
 Additionally, selecting a fixed core in the origin tree proposed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "subsec:Fixed-nodes"

\end_inset

 as extension to the TED, as well as a tree unification algorithm in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

