\begin_layout Paragraph
Black Box: SARSA
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to avoid an involuntary selection process, the top entry of the
 OpenAI leaderboard was chosen as black box.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
name "https://github.com/openai/gym/wiki/Leaderboard"
target "https://github.com/openai/gym/wiki/Leaderboard"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The featured approach uses SARSA with tiling and a neural network and achieves
 the goal of reaching the target in less than 110 steps on average after
 75 training episodes with an average reward of 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$-103.41$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xiao2019"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 At this stage, the agent is still very inexperienced, sometimes even failing
 to climb the hill in time (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sarsa75_problem_decision_plots"

\end_inset

).
 Such an error is hardly noticeable in practice and exemplifies the risk
 of blindly trusting a black box.
 However, since the agent's training was stopped as early as possible, this
 agent would never be used in security-relevant areas.
 Therefore, as a more authentic black box policy, a more experienced version
 was trained with 200 training episodes.
 As a result, the problems mentioned above hardly matter anymore, and the
 reward also drops by almost 5 to 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$
\backslash
approx-98.7$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sarsa-200-decision-plots"

\end_inset

).
 
