\begin_layout Subsection
IB Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The regression error could, at its best, not be further reduced than 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$
\backslash
approx 2$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 for MSE runs and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

$
\backslash
approx 1.5$
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in MAE runs.
 Action 0 is the most difficult to approximate (compare with 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:ib-plots"

\end_inset

); the error was (on average) 30% higher than for the rest.
 Compared to candidates in the MC benchmark, this is worse by a factor of
 5 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:Histograms-mc-ib"

\end_inset

).
 This is also confirmed by the results found by Hein in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, where the regression error also did not fall significantly below a limit
 of 2.
 This means, that the PSONN black box policy could not be sufficiently approxima
ted.
 Since the candidates cannot expect any performance gain, their evaluation
 would not be meaningful.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Familiar GP candidates have no performance advantages compared to regular
 ones, despite their enhanced familiarity, both regression error and the
 fitness did not distinguish significantly.
 Comparing online performance arguably does not make sense when the approximatio
n of the model did not converge.
 However, familiarity was conserved without falling short to regular GP.
 Otherwise, symbolic regression is not applicable on such sparse transition
 samples.
\end_layout

