\begin_layout Section
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the experiments, extensions were found for all expert strategies, with
 familiar structures clearly preserved.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Compared to regular GP results, familiar candidates achieved a similar regressio
n error, but differed significantly in both the genotype (the tree structure)
 and phenotype (the behaviour), which were more similar to the origin program.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The benefits of familiarity for interpretability are difficult to quantify.
 A very simple mathematical formula can be much more difficult to understand
 than a comprehensive strategy that is intuitive, as can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:if-operator-composition"

\end_inset

.
 The human approach is to abstract a problem and divide it into several,
 well-aranged tasks.
 Regular GP finds simple formulae that are well optimizable with random
 mutation.
 The If-condition shows the contradiction between interpretability and number
 of nodes clearly; with 3 inputs, it has the largest branching factor, but
 it breaks down the understanding process as every child branch can be considere
d separately and interpreted more easily as isolated chunk.
 Familiar GP also looks for the simplest possible extensions, but the default
 through the core approach tends to lead to the use of the same basic reasoning.
\end_layout

