\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Three formulas with completely different approaches that achieve almost
 identical results.
 The If-operator in the first formula provides good interpretability and
 confidence by dividing the problem into two cases.
 The second formula calculates the action directly from the velocity and
 position of the cart, making it harder to understand.
 The last formula ultimately only requires 3 nodes, but overfits the problem
 and is hardly comprehensible.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:if-operator-composition"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Minor difficulties were encountered in runs with simple strategies, where
 a few edits can lead to decay of the kernel structure due to the small
 number of nodes.
 A solution to this problem was achieved by manually defining a fixed core.
 Defining fixed nodes also had a positive effect in complex origin trees,
 by significantly reducing the runtime.
 The constraints through fixed knots, as well as other limitations (e.g.,
 a reduced set of operators, exclusion of actions), did not have a major
 impact on the regression error.
 On the contrary, it even had positive effects by creating a more homogeneous
 population, allowing for a better comparison between individual candidates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The TED is well suited as a surrogate measure of familiarity, yet some anomalies
 may occur.
 The complexity and interpretability of a formula especially depicts the
 operators of a tree, while the terminal nodes have a comparatively insignifican
t role.
 Changes to constants in particular fall heavily as full-fledged edits,
 although their optimization process is actually desired.
 This can prevent deliberate simplifications and even lead to artificial
 constructions of operators around the constants in order to protect their
 values.
 Observations are less affected by this problem, but have another comparatively
 important role in complexity, as the size of their set represents the dimension
 of the input vector.
 Taking into account the disproportionate effect of terminal nodes, we propose
 to trim the TED; a simple solution is to exclude constant nodes when computing
 the TED.
 Furthermore, the symmetric distance between the sets of observations can
 be considered to fully exclude terminal nodes from the edit distance.
 Also, it is recommended to unify and order computational trees before calculati
ng the TED, as was proposed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "sec:sympy-tree-reduction"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A general limitation in symbolic regression is the ability to sufficiently
 approximate the hidden strategy of a black box with a function.
 For example, in the case of the PSONN policy used in the IB, the strategy
 was too sparse, so the regression error was insufficiently reduced in both
 the regular and familiar runs, which was already been described by Hein
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "daniel_hein_doktorarbeit"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
 To a lesser extent, this problem also occurred with the SARSA-75 policy,
 for which the regression error was more difficult to reduce than with the
 SARSA-200 version, which followed a better but also simpler strategy.
 It is also recommended to consider the regression error of the origin program
 before starting a run, as a large difference indicates that the strategies
 are fundamentally different and might be incompatible with each other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Overall, the positive effects of familiarity were many, allowing for better
 interpretability and guiding the search for a conceivably difficult formula
 without limiting performance.
 
\end_layout

