\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In this thesis, a novel technique to create familiar reinforcement learning
 policies was proposed by extending an expert program with genetic programming
 to function like a black box policy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Domain experts programs represent the best human effort to solve a problem
 and are thus a anchor point for interpretability and trust.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This approach was implemented in three steps.
 First, a previously developed strategy must be represented as computational
 tree.
 Second, transition samples tree edit distance using the edit distance to
 the familiar solution as complexity metric in a GP process.
 Additionally, a fixed core can be selected manually to further protect
 familiar structures in programs.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this thesis, familiarity was introduced as concept for a novel method
 to create interpretable RL policies based on prior known expert strategies.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Domain experts programs represent an anchor point for interpretability and
 trust, which are extended with genetic programming to function like a black
 box policy.
 This is achieved by using the edit distance to the origin program as complexity
 criterion in a genetic programming process.
 Additionally, the user can select fixed nodes to explicitly protect tree
 structures.
 Familiar candidates are then created by performing a symbolic regression
 on pre-collected black box transition samples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The approach was evaluated on the mountain car and the industrial benchmark,
 covering a variety of expert programs, black box policies and further restricti
ons.
 For all runs, several candidates were found in which the respective expert
 strategies were clearly recognizable.
 Selecting a fixed core allows a very specific protection of structures,
 which showed great effect especially in simple programs.
 Compared to regular runs, no negative effect on the regression error was
 observed.
 However, the specifications led to a more homogeneous Pareto front, which
 facilitated a direct comparison of the candidates and resulted in fewer
 negative outliers in the online performance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The proposed methods to create familiar RL policies have shown to enhance
 interpretability without sacrificing performance.
 In addition, opportunities arise through guiding the optimization by targeted
 preservation of relevant structures to efficiently create strategies that
 are relatively complex.
\end_layout

