\begin_layout Subsection
Differentiating and Weighting the Familiarity of Edits 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "TED-improvements"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By using a distance measure such as the edit distance, the question emerges
 whether (and how) the nature of a change can be taken into account.
 Weighting nodes with different complexities is a straightforward approach
 in regular GP, a sample weighting of the commercial GP Framework 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
emph{Eureqa}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "tab:EUREKA-GP-complexities"

\end_inset

.
 To apply a similar weighting to an edit distance, the weighted mapping
 must be computed, since a subsequent weighting of the regular mapping is
 not necessarily the minimum distance.
 A simple answer to the question of whether and how edits should be rated
 is to ignore the altered nodes.
 Another idea is to divide operators into classes, with weighted changes,
 a potential implementation can be seen in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:operator-classification"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

